  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "A catastrophic collapse erased a canyon overnight. In March 1928, California's brand-new St. Francis Dam suddenly gave way, unleashing a deadly flood.",
        "visual_query": "historic california canyon landscape",
        "visual_fallback": "dry rocky canyon"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Twelve billion gallons of reservoir surged into the darkness, sweeping away houses, bridges, and entire families without warning.",
        "visual_query": "rushing river flood water",
        "visual_fallback": "rushing water"
      },
      {
        "narration": "How does solid concrete just dissolve? Chief engineer William Mulholland had staked his legendary reputation on this massive curved wall.",
        "visual_query": "engineer blueprint historical office",
        "visual_fallback": "vintage blueprint"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The terrifying truth wasn't bad cement. The dam's western abutment was built directly onto an ancient, prehistoric landslide that turned to mush when soaked.",
        "visual_query": "geological rock strata cliff",
        "visual_fallback": "rock cliff"
      },
      {
        "narration": "When the foundation slaked like dry clay, the monolith sheared in half. It remains the deadliest civil engineering failure in American history.",
        "visual_query": "historic dam ruins concrete",
        "visual_fallback": "ruined concrete structure"
      }
    ],
